FREE


 Book donations sponsored by Candlewick Press
 Co-presented with San Francisco Public Library
 &nbsp;
 Our acclaimed Kidquake programs for children’s books; authors, illustrators, poets, and bookmakers provide readings, discussions, and workshops designed to fuel kids’ imagination. This morning’s two sessions, for kids in 3rd to 5th grades, begin at 10:15am and 11:15am. Featuring authors&nbsp;Dan Santat, Lisa Moore Ramée, plus a mid-presentation performance by author Tiffany Golden, with workshops (by lottery) from author & illustrator of Agent Cupcake&nbsp;Mel Hilario&nbsp;& Katie Longua&nbsp;(comics), author/illustrator of The Paper Princess&nbsp;Elisa Kleven (collage), and 826 Valencia (podcasting). Advance sign-up required for in-person student groups. Streamed live at youtube.com/SanFranciscoLibrary.&nbsp;

Pay-what-you-can for guaranteed seat; FREE to walk-ups (space depending)

Sponsored by Yerba Buena Partnership

Humor is one of a writer’s best tools, and an oft-underutilized one at that. More than just a hook to get their attention, a well-timed joke can break down a reader’s defenses faster than almost anything else on the page. Want to sharpen your wit-wielding skills? Join&nbsp;Claire Oshetsky,&nbsp;Rebecca Handler,&nbsp;Scott Meyer, and&nbsp;Malik Robinson&nbsp;as they unpack their secrets of how humor can raise the stakes, heighten the drama, and make both tragic and comedic moments hit twice as hard. Moderated by&nbsp;Yuliya Patsay.&nbsp;

Pay-what-you-can for guaranteed seat; FREE to walk-ups (space depending)

Sponsored by Yerba Buena Partnership

When the world's on fire, storytelling can act as first-responder to community hearts and minds, inspiring real-world activism that drives social change. From our expert authors, you’ll learn how to shine a light on things that matter and craft narratives that build genuine empathy, explore cultural and social responsibility, and maybe, just maybe, motivate readers to act. Join panelists&nbsp;Jilanne Hoffmann,&nbsp;Khan Wong,&nbsp;Safia Elhillo, and&nbsp;Savala Nolan&nbsp;for tips spanning genres across poetry, longform essays, children’s books, and speculative fiction. Moderated by&nbsp;Jane Ganahl.&nbsp;

Pay-what-you-can for guaranteed seat; FREE to walk-ups (space depending)

Sponsored by Yerba Buena Partnership

We’ve all heard about “show, don’t tell” but how, exactly, does a writer show the most evocative details possible? Let's lean into the heat, the ache, the sensory overload. In this panel,&nbsp;Jennifer Barone,&nbsp;Sandra Jackson-Opoku, and&nbsp;Tomas Moniz&nbsp;will show you how to write about our most unspoken longings (the sensual, the emotional, and the taboo) and they're not afraid to go there! Moderated by&nbsp;Tijanna Eaton.&nbsp;

$20 adv / $25 door

Co-presented with The Stud

Literary Speakeasy returns to Litquake for another hysterical evening of Bay Area drag superstars putting on their reading glasses to celebrate the most scandalous, most outrageous, and most ridiculous celebrity memoirs and autobiographies! From&nbsp;Real Housewives&nbsp;to Hollywood legends, no book or celebrity is off limits for&nbsp;Fagatha Christie,&nbsp;Lisa Frankenstein,&nbsp;Vanilla Meringue,&nbsp;Piranha Psychotronica,&nbsp;BeBe Sweetbriar, and&nbsp;Laundra Thyme. Come join your hosts&nbsp;James J. Siegel&nbsp;and&nbsp;Elsa Touche&nbsp;for an evening of laughs, queer camp, and prizes! Doors at 5:30pm.&nbsp;

$15 gen admission / $45 with signed book

Co-presented with Kearny Street Workshop

The National Book Award finalist and New York Times bestselling author of Pachinko returns with a breathtaking contemporary epic. In American Hagwon,&nbsp;Min Jin Lee has crafted an unforgettable, panoramic novel where the smallest of gestures can have enormous repercussions. Once comfortably middle class in Korea, John and Helen Koh and their three children—Bo, DH, and Mido—find their lives upended, first by a shocking betrayal by John’s oldest friend, then by the 1997 Asian financial crisis. Desperately striving to regain their footing, they leave Seoul for Sydney and eventually settle in Southern California—where new vistas of opportunity open up for the children as their parents, strangers in a strange land, must adjust to a new life in which their experience and education mean little. In conversation with Andrew Sean Greer. Doors at 6:00pm.&nbsp;

Pay-what-you-can for guaranteed seat; FREE to walk-ups (space depending)

Sponsored by Yerba Buena Partnership

Querying agents and waiting on slush piles isn't the only way to get published. For many writers it's not even the best one. In this panel, successful indie authors&nbsp;Tara Dorabji, Marianne Lonsdale, Regina Louise,&nbsp;and&nbsp;Wendy Johnson&nbsp;pull back the curtain on surprising, alternative paths such as contest contracts, hybrid publishers, and DIY presses to get stories published and promoted. Moderated by indie author&nbsp;Matthew Félix.&nbsp;

Pay-what-you-can for guaranteed seat; FREE to walk-ups (space depending)

Sponsored by Yerba Buena Partnership

The launch party is over, the champagne is flat—now what? Getting your book out into the world is only the beginning. In this session, editor/publisher&nbsp;Julia Park Tracey&nbsp;will moderate a conversation between jack-of-all-genres&nbsp;Ariel Gore, bookseller&nbsp;Jasmine Valandani, and memoirist&nbsp;Mary Alice Stephens&nbsp;about what comes after the launch: how to keep your book visible, cultivate both new and lifelong readers, and keep things moving without burning yourself out.&nbsp;

FREE, $10-$15 suggested donation

Co-presented with SFPL

From the author of Queen Sugar comes a piercing examination of friendship and loyalty&nbsp;set in a tight-knit enclave in the Bay Area as two best friends find themselves at odds over their beliefs on race, identity, and allyship, especially when it comes to their teenage children. Babs and Nora have spent a decade’s worth of morning walks sharing family secrets, local drama, and advice—everything from tips on lingerie choices to Babs’s suggestions to Nora, a white woman, on how to raise Nathan, her adopted Black son. But their worlds are turned upside down when the cops are called on their kids, leading to a traumatic police encounter for Nathan. Torn between respecting Nora’s boundaries and embodying the values so important to her own family, Babs will make a choice that will shape their lives forever. In conversation with filmmaker and Jingletown Films’ founder Cheryl Dunye. Doors at 2:30pm.&nbsp;

Pay-what-you-can for guaranteed seat; FREE to walk-ups (space depending)

Co-presented with American Bookbinders Museum

A notoriously precise and difficult form, the short story occupies an odd place in arts & letters in 2026. Left mostly by the wayside by the publishing industry at large, the story still holds a prominent place in our biggest magazines and workshop culture. Local purveyors of the craft Rucy Cui, Cristina Fríes, and William Lohier read from their celebrated work and discuss the form's place in today's publishing/reading culture. Moderated by Litquake Artistic Director Graham Todd. Doors at 7:00pm.&nbsp;

$5 MI members / $15 gen public

Co-presented with Museum of the African Diaspora & Mechanics’ Institute&nbsp;

Join Zinzi Clemmons&nbsp;in conversation with Faith Adiele on Clemmons's latest essay collection, Freedom, a deft mix of memoir, family history, criticism, and reportage, drawing on a vast range of material from Joan Didion to James Baldwin, political analysis and history to Clemmons’s own experiences across the globe. In elegiac prose, Clemmons trains her discerning eye on American institutions and mythologies, probing the bounds of liberation and autonomy to interrogate our most enduring quest—the relentless pursuit of freedom for all. Doors at 6:00pm.

$16

Co-presented with ZYZZYVA and the Roxie

For this festival installment of our year-round film series,&nbsp;Andrew Sean Greer&nbsp;introduces a special screening of&nbsp;Amarcord, the dramatic comedy directed by Federico Fellini. Fellini recreates his hometown of Rimini in this carnivalesque film, where the director satirizes his childhood. Winner of an Academy Award for best foreign-language film,&nbsp;Amarcord&nbsp;remains one of the director’s best-loved creations, beautifully weaving together Giuseppe Rotunno’s colorful cinematography, Danilo Donati’s extravagant costumes and sets, and Nino Rota’s nostalgia-tinged score. Following the screening, Greer will appear in conversation with&nbsp;Ingrid Rojas Contreras&nbsp;about his film selection and most recent book,&nbsp;Villa Coco,&nbsp;in which a young man takes an unspecified job with a charismatic elderly baronessa at her crumbling villa in the Tuscan hills. Co-hosted by&nbsp;ZYZZYVA&nbsp;editor&nbsp;Oscar Villalon. Doors at 12:00pm for book sales/signing.&nbsp;

FREE, $10-15 suggested donation

Co-presented with City Lights

Master of literary horror Brian Evenson returns with a long-awaited follow-up to his 2016 novel, Last Days. After destroying the headquarters of an underground amputation cult, a burned and battered Kline finds himself in a holding cell. When he enlists the help of an ex-cop to commute his prison sentence, Kline inadvertently tangles with a schism of the very cult he sought to destroy. Can Kline investigate the sect’s ritualistic murder spree without getting lured into their ranks? Frigid and precise, Phantom Limb interrogates our notions of wholeness, fate, and the lengths we are willing to go to connect with a higher power. In conversation with Colin Winnette.&nbsp;

FREE, $10-$15 suggested donation


 Co-presented with Museum of the African Diaspora


 From the author of the multi-award-winning, National Book Award–longlisted, “vivid, fast, funny, way-smart, and verbally inventive” (George Saunders) story collection Heads of the Colored People,&nbsp;comes a sly, spry, literary debut novel.&nbsp;We find ourselves in 1920s Oklahoma, just far enough out of Tulsa, just long enough after the Race Massacre of 1921. For as long as anyone can remember, the Milfords have led plentiful lives on the backs of the townspeople of Newville. On the ominous brink of the Great Depression and at the height of Prohibition, Richard Milford is an infamous moonshiner and womanizer who, it seems, finally crossed the wrong person at the wrong time. He’s dead and buried with only his women to mourn him, but a question remains: Who killed him, and why? Top suspects are his four “wives,” but as their stories burst to light, the very idea of a true story comes apart before our eyes. In conversation with Margaret Wilkerson Sexton. Doors at 5:30pm.&nbsp;

FREE, $10-$15 suggested donation

Sponsored by Norway House and Center for the Art of Translation
 Part of Words Around the World&nbsp;

From International Booker Prize– and National Book Award–nominated author Vigdis Hjorth comes her latest novel finally translated into English, Repetition. As winter approaches in Norway and the daylight dwindles, a chance encounter prompts a novelist to reexamine her past. The seismic events following her sixteenth birthday return with haunting vividness, exposing a story both utterly familiar and desperately strange. It was the year she first got drunk, the year she first had sex with a boy. She was watched like a hawk by an anxious mother and a silent, distant father. It was a year of typical teenage fixation and typical teenage frivolity, and of all the usual parental fretting. Until something else took hold, and her family made an unspoken decision and a terrible sacrifice. Only now, decades later, can these events come close to being comprehended. In conversation with Daniela Blei.&nbsp;

$15 / $3 early bird discount until October 18th (code: earlybird16)

Co-presented with Locus Magazine and SF in SF

Celebrate the spooky season with&nbsp;Ellen Datlow, Hugo Award–winning editor of the new horror anthology&nbsp;All Hallows Eve: Stories of Halloween and Other Festivals of the Dead, a chilling lineup of 19 original short stories delving into our worldwide obsession with that time of year when the veil is lifted, spirits come alive, and the boundary between the living and the dead is far too thin. Joined by anthology contributors and world-class masters of the genre&nbsp;Linda D. Addison,&nbsp;Theresa DeLucci,&nbsp;Brian Evenson, and&nbsp;Jeffrey Ford, Datlow will offer a rare peek behind the publishing curtain, introducing the authors reading from their featured works with insights on how each one was hand-picked for inclusion in her anthology. But that’s not all—Datlow will also sit down with&nbsp;Locus Magazine&nbsp;editor-in-chief&nbsp;Liza Trombi&nbsp;to discuss four-plus decades of her legendary career in the horror genre, and the countless authors she’s shepherded to print. Doors at 7:30pm.&nbsp;

FREE, $10-$15 suggested donation


 Sponsored by Norway House
 Part of Words Around the World&nbsp;


 From International Booker Prize– and National Book Award–nominated author Vigdis Hjorth comes her latest novel finally translated into English, Repetition. As winter approaches in Norway and the daylight dwindles, a chance encounter prompts a novelist to reexamine her past. The seismic events following her sixteenth birthday return with haunting vividness, exposing a story both utterly familiar and desperately strange. It was the year she first got drunk, the year she first had sex with a boy. She was watched like a hawk by an anxious mother and a silent, distant father. It was a year of typical teenage fixation and typical teenage frivolity, and of all the usual parental fretting. Until something else took hold, and her family made an unspoken decision and a terrible sacrifice. Only now, decades later, can these events come close to being comprehended. In conversation with Britta Stromeyer.&nbsp;

FREE, $10-15 suggested donation


 Co-presented with The Stud

In celebration of Hieu Minh Nguyen's third poetry collection, Staying Still, join Litquake and The Stud for a night of readings and drag performances that highlight the power and resonance of stillness. Featuring drag from Pam, Hera Wynn, Mary Vice, and Hands. Readings from Brittany Newell, Hieu Minh Nguyen, Brontez Purnell, and Garrett Schlichte. Hosted by curator Micheal Foulk and Hands. Doors at 7:00 PM.&nbsp;

FREE, $10–15 suggested donation, $33 for signed book

Co-presented with SFPL

In the latest addition to the inimitable Walter Mosley’s &nbsp;Joe King Oliver series, King finds himself accepting (again at his grandmother’s request) a case from Arman Babylon, a powerful Los Angeles gangster now dying from cancer. Babylon’s granddaughter, Twyla Mann, has been missing for years, and he’s desperate to find her before he passes. The search pulls King into the Babylons’ stained past and the cold case of Dalia Shorts, a torch singer who vanished without a trace and whom Arman was once suspected of killing. As King follows her trail from L.A. to New York and back, he’ll realize nothing is what it seems as he uncovers a pattern of violence that has targeted the women around Babylon for decades. How much will King be willing to risk to finally set things right? In conversation with Carvell Wallace. Doors at 6:00pm.&nbsp;
